{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing, the model under-forecasts levels across most of the 30-day horizon (often by several €/t). Apply an upward level shift when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model reacts too slowly to bullish reversals (e.g., after a local low). Heuristic: if price has rebounded strongly from a recent trough, steepen the first 10–15 forecast days upward (increase slope), not just the level.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: errors grow with horizon in rallies—near-term (days 1–5) is closer, but days ~10–30 are increasingly too low. Apply a ramped correction that increases with lead time during uptrends.",
    "Over-mean-reversion / capped upside: forecasts tend to revert toward mid-60s even when the market breaks higher (true outcomes move into high-60s/near-70). In breakout conditions (new 20–30d highs), reduce mean-reversion strength and allow higher terminal levels.",
    "Volatility compression: the model’s forecast path is too smooth and misses the magnitude of sustained moves. When realized 10–15d volatility is elevated, widen the forecast distribution and increase expected move size (especially upward in bullish momentum)."
  ]
}
